article.articleSummary:
- Dengue fever fatalities reach 15 in Sumatra Selatan in 2026 through August, with two siblings in Penukal Abab Lematang Ilir (PALI) dead and under investigation. The Sumatera Selatan Health Office (Dinkes) has instructed local Dinkes and Puskesmas in PALI to verify the cases and conduct an epidemiological investigation, reviewing disease progression, lab results, treatment history, and cause of death. Field teams will search for dengue cases around the victims’ residence, inspect larvae (jentik), and identify potential Aedes breeding sites, tracing victims’ activities and mobility prior to illness. Officials caution that no visible standing water does not rule out breeding sites, which can include small containers around homes. Fogging will be considered only after epidemiological results; 
- Two siblings, a 9-year-old boy Andika Alam Saputra and a 7-year-old girl Hulya Aliya Felis, died of dengue fever (DBD) in Handayani Mulya, Talang Ubi District, PALI Regency, South Sumatra. Both deaths were dengue-positive. Community leader Habibi reports no fogging has been conducted in the area since the fatalities, despite repeated reports to Dinas Kesehatan PALI and the Talang Ubi Health Center to treat standing-water sites and conduct fogging. RT 04 RW 01 urges immediate fogging and environmental inspections to prevent further cases. Community criticism centers on slow public-health response and access to care, noting referrals to hospitals outside PALI. As of 26 August 2026, health authorities have not issued a statement or confirmed any response actions.
- Two siblings, Hulya Aliya Felis (7) and Andika Alam Saputra (9), died within days in South Sumatra after dengue fever. Hulya fell ill with fever, weakness, and loss of appetite while hospitalized at Rumah Sakit Talang Ubi in Penukal Abab Lematang Ilir (PALI). With deterioration, she was referred to Rumah Sakit Muara Enim, where blood tests confirmed dengue fever. Her brother Andika, initially presenting with fever and weakness, insisted on accompanying her; en route to Muara Enim he developed shortness of breath and lost consciousness, dying soon after arrival. The family, led by Antap Mustofa, mourns both children’s sudden deaths. The report notes laboratory-confirmed dengue in Hulya but does not specify the infection source or other cases in the community.
